  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 131 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThe pattern becomes active once again during the latter half of the \nwork week. By Thursday morning, an upper-level shortwave approaches \nwest Texas and southeast New Mexico. Shower and thunderstorm chances \nbegin to increase Thursday morning across southernmost portions of \nthe area, spreading northward throughout the day and into Friday as \nthe system moves across the region. Chances during this timeframe \nstill generally range between 40-70% across the area (up to 80%). \nWhereas this past weekend the highest chances tended to be across \nthe western half of the area, by Thursday and Friday the best \nchances will be across the eastern half (ie from the Big Bend \nnorthward into the Lower Trans-Pecos and much of the Permian Basin \nof west Texas). PWATs will once again be between the 90-99th \npercentile (pushing daily maximum values), so flash flooding is \ngoing to be our main concern, especially for easternmost counties \nwhere confidence in widespread showers and thunderstorms remains \nhighest. \n\nBy Friday evening/overnight, most of this activity pushes beyond our \narea. Guidance continues to show most locations staying dry for \nHalloween. Another approaching system may bring additional rain \nchances to portions of the area by the end of the weekend/beginning \nof next week, but chances are low overall at this time (10-30%). \nBoth the ECMWF and the GFS (and their ensembles) attempt to bring \nyet another system towards the area by the early-to-middle part of \nnext week. This system looks like it would be drier, but it could \npush a front our direction and yield cooler temperatures. Details as \nto just how much cooler it could get remain uncertain this far in \nadvance, so stay tuned for updates! Otherwise, highs through the \nweekend mainly top out in the mid-to-upper 70s, except on Friday \n(highs stay in the upper 60s and low-to-mid 70s thanks to rain \nchances and cloud cover).\n\nSprang",
